<details>
    <summary>Backport Instructions</summary>

* :mag: Use `git blame` to find the most recent pull request in the affected project that updated the affected component. For Go dependencies, this will normally be the version corresponding to the dependency in go.mod, and system dependencies this will be the base image in the Dockerfile (i.e. the line beginning `FROM ghcr.io/github/gh-base-image/gh-base-...`).
* :label: Add the `ern:not-customer-facing` label to the pull request, along with the `enterprise-x.y-backport` labels for every version starting at the newest going to the oldest affected release.
* :robot: Wait for the backport pull requests to be opened (they'll reference the original PR so will show up in its timeline). If there are merge conflicts on updates to the Dockerfile they will be resolved automatically, otherwise you will need to resolve them manually.
* :pencil2: Fill in the backport PR template info
  * You can mark it as low risk and use a comment like one of these for the "Why" section:
    > Updates dependencies in Docker image to resolve container scanning vulnerabilities.
    > Updates Go dependencies to resolve container scanning vulnerabilities.
* :heavy_check_mark: Check that the "Enterprise Tests" CI job passes on the backport pull requests then approve them.
* :checkered_flag: Merge the pull request.

</details>

